    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d527">

                <pb facs="0740" n="664" xml:id="pg664"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5331/138: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Portugal</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-04-05T17:00:00-05:00">April 5, 1935—5 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3. Your 5, April 4, 11 a.m.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d527-f1"/> Department’s 2, March
                    30, 6 p.m., specifically states that the Belgian concessions will be extended to
                    Portugal so long as we are bound by the provisions of the existing commercial
                    arrangement between the two countries. Although we are considering giving notice
                    of termination of this commercial arrangement, we have not yet done so. Please
                    see the appropriate authorities and explain that the concessions to Belgium will
                    for the present be extended to Portugal.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d527-f1" n="9" xml:id="d527-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that the newspapers merely published brief factual
                    accounts of the exclusion of Portugal from benefits from the Belgian treaty.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
